Security Groups vs NACL — what’s the deal?

Think of Security Groups as the locks on your doors. 
NACLs are the fences around your house.

Security Groups protect your EC2 instances.
NACLs protect your subnets.

A Security Group controls who can reach your server.
It’s stateful — if traffic comes in, the response goes out automatically.
You’ll use it daily in DevOps setups.

A NACL filters traffic at the subnet level.
It’s stateless — you need to allow both inbound and outbound separately.
It’s helpful for fine control.

Security Groups = your default
NACLs = your extra shield
